      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [2] explains the formula: a new worker is launched every autovacuum_naptime/N seconds. With N=4 and naptime=60, 60/4 = 15 seconds.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "(Therefore, if the installation has N databases, a new worker will be launched every autovacuum_naptime/N seconds.)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Each database gets at most one worker at a time.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [3] explicitly states there is no limit on how many workers might be in a single database.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "There is no limit on how many workers might be in a single database"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Running workers count against max_connections.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [3] explicitly states that running workers do not count towards max_connections limits.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "Note that the number of running workers does not count towards max_connections or superuser_reserved_connections limits."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Autovacuum processes partitions like ordinary tables, but it does not run ANALYZE on partitioned tables.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [5] states that autovacuum processes table partitions like other tables but does not run ANALYZE on partitioned tables.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "(Autovacuum does process table partitions just like other tables.) Unfortunately, this means that autovacuum does not run ANALYZE on partitioned tables"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "It does not issue ANALYZE for foreign tables either.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [7] states that the autovacuum daemon does not issue ANALYZE commands for foreign tables.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 7,
          "quote": "The autovacuum daemon does not issue ANALYZE commands for foreign tables"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Normally a conflicting lock request interrupts autovacuum, and the same is true for an autovacuum that is running to prevent transaction ID wraparound.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage [6] states that while normal autovacuum is interrupted by conflicting locks, autovacuum running to prevent transaction ID wraparound is NOT automatically interrupted.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "However, if the autovacuum is running to prevent transaction ID wraparound ... the autovacuum is not automatically interrupted."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

